WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.360
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:07.860
ARUN SUBRAMANIYAN: OK.

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:08.800
Good afternoon.

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:10.400
Thanks for coming.

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:11.640
I'm Arun Subramaniyan.

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:13.590
I lead the data science
and analytics team

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:16.120
at Baker Hughes, a GE company.

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:21.420
We'll talk today about how we
deal with analytics at scale

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:23.130
for the industrial world.

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:26.250
Specifically for oil and
gas, but more in general,

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:28.500
what are the scalable
aspects and what

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:30.830
are the commonalities
of analytics

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:32.610
in the industrial world.

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:34.410
And just to give
you a little bit

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:36.750
of an introduction of
the kinds of problems

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:38.010
that we're interested in--

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:42.570
and by we, I mean the larger GE,
so it is the larger GE company.

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:44.820
So for those of
you who don't know,

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:47.370
every two seconds an
airplane takes off

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:50.720
somewhere in the world
with a GE engine on it.

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:54.200
About 30% of the
world's power is

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:58.960
generated by a GE turbine
of one kind or the other.

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:01.520
And we formed Baker
Hughes, a GE company,

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:05.960
last July by merging GE oil
and gas with Baker Hughes, two

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:09.860
companies which, by their own
merits, were large [INAUDIBLE]

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:10.970
their own merits.

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:14.360
But the merger brought
together the first,

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:16.750
world's first full-stream
oil and gas company.

00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:18.620
By that, we mean
the first company

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:22.100
in the world that can
do exploration for oil,

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:26.730
extract oil, ship the oil,
refine it, and then do

00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:28.730
something with the
refined products.

00:01:28.730 --> 00:01:31.270
So make energy, make something
else with the products.

00:01:31.270 --> 00:01:33.180
The only company,
the first company

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:35.880
in the world that
can do that at scale.

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:39.210
Now, to do any of these
individual things by itself

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:40.560
is a big feat.

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:44.220
Doing all of these things
simultaneously in one company

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:47.952
requires us to build a
lot of models repeatedly.

00:01:47.952 --> 00:01:49.410
And I've not even
touched upon what

00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:52.320
we do in the health care space
or in the transportation space,

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:52.870
for example.

00:01:52.870 --> 00:01:54.880
These are just simple examples.

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:58.320
And to do this at
scale, daily we

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:00.150
have to build
thousands of models.

00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:03.090
And for us, models are
any representations

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:06.000
that connect a set of
inputs to a set of outputs.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:08.747
Now, that can be as simple
as a simple equation,

00:02:08.747 --> 00:02:10.830
or a full-blown partial
differential equation that

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:13.700
takes about two months to
solve on the world's fastest

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:15.240
supercomputer.

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:18.120
All of those things
for us is a model.

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:21.390
Now, to do this
effectively, we, of course,

00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:24.060
need the raw material
for it, which is data.

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:25.980
And we deal with
data in two ways.

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:29.270
So we have what we call two
big dichotomies of silos.

00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:31.070
The first silo is data.

00:02:31.070 --> 00:02:33.280
The second silo is
analytics, or second silo

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:35.015
is subject matter expertise.

00:02:35.015 --> 00:02:37.800
I'll first tackle
the data silos.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:41.730
And if you look at across the
entire industrial spectrum,

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:45.260
the first one, of course,
is oil and gas here listed.

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:48.110
From there to
aviation, say, take

00:02:48.110 --> 00:02:51.390
an aircraft that takes off
from New York, lands in London.

00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:53.650
The aircraft was designed
about 20 years ago.

00:02:53.650 --> 00:02:56.090
The engines are not
collecting that much data.

00:02:56.090 --> 00:02:58.880
That's roughly about a
terabyte of data generated.

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:01.490
Not much of it is stored,
not much of it is analyzed.

00:03:01.490 --> 00:03:04.160
But today's newest aircraft
generate about a terabyte

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:06.950
per hour of flight.

00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:10.670
Take that in the oil and gas
world, where an offshore oil

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:14.480
rig, for the past 30
years, has been generating

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:18.590
roughly 15,000 to 20,000
tags worth of data every two

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:19.690
seconds.

00:03:19.690 --> 00:03:23.080
And almost all of
it has been stored.

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:26.980
Compared to the power or
transportation or the aviation

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:29.230
industry, the oil
and gas industry

00:03:29.230 --> 00:03:31.900
has at least a couple
of orders of magnitude

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:34.840
more in terms of data,
primarily because they

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:37.210
had to deal with
uncertainty from the get-go.

00:03:37.210 --> 00:03:39.550
There's very little you
know about the reservoirs.

00:03:39.550 --> 00:03:42.310
There's very little you can
actually model or control

00:03:42.310 --> 00:03:44.350
about what happens subsurface.

00:03:44.350 --> 00:03:46.600
Whereas an engine,
it's complex, it's

00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:48.550
extraordinarily
sophisticated, but you

00:03:48.550 --> 00:03:51.470
control almost everything around
that particular [INAUDIBLE]..

00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:54.611
So the uncertainties exist,
but they're far smaller.

00:03:54.611 --> 00:03:56.860
So the data sets that you
see in the oil and gas world

00:03:56.860 --> 00:03:59.950
significantly outstrip anything
else in the industrial world.

00:03:59.950 --> 00:04:04.550
But if you compare that to any
data sets in the ERP systems--

00:04:04.550 --> 00:04:07.120
traditional ERP world-- for
the last 20 years, the last 30

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:10.030
years where you think about
any transactional data

00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:12.160
sets, any kind of
maintenance records,

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:14.200
any kind of
information that you've

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:17.120
been sharing and
storing and retrieving

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:20.410
in the traditional
enterprise world

00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:24.220
gets dwarfed by the industrial
data set by at least two

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:26.500
to five orders of
magnitude, both in terms

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:29.550
of volumes and velocities.

00:04:29.550 --> 00:04:33.030
And there are no systems that
have been developed that would

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:38.260
tackle something at that scale,
even the modern industrial--

00:04:38.260 --> 00:04:40.650
or the internet 2.0
kinds of technologies,

00:04:40.650 --> 00:04:43.830
where we track everything that
everybody's doing in this room,

00:04:43.830 --> 00:04:46.000
everybody that's doing
anywhere in the world--

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:47.500
where they are,
where they're going,

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:49.410
what they're probably
going to do--

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:52.560
it still gets dwarfed
significantly.

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:55.870
And I'll give you some examples
of how we go about solving it,

00:04:55.870 --> 00:04:58.170
but this is just one
side of the story.

00:04:58.170 --> 00:05:00.300
The data silo is one
side of the story

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:01.800
that everybody talks about.

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:04.110
The other side of the
story is the analytic silo,

00:05:04.110 --> 00:05:06.570
or the subject
matter expert silos,

00:05:06.570 --> 00:05:08.820
where people have been
building analytics for decades.

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:12.690
People have been building
analytics for 50 years.

00:05:12.690 --> 00:05:15.270
If we have the luxury
of going and building

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:18.420
a model from scratch, from
a data set that you still

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:20.940
have to prepare painstakingly,
that's a great thing.

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:24.610
You have a lot of frameworks
out there today to go build.

00:05:24.610 --> 00:05:29.220
But if you had models that have
been in production for decades,

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:32.850
that people understood, have
been using it in production,

00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:35.490
today folks who are using
it may not necessarily

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:37.580
understand all the
nuances of the model.

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:39.330
You don't necessarily
want to go touch it.

00:05:39.330 --> 00:05:41.850
You don't necessarily want
to go tweak it or change it.

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:43.230
You want to be careful about it.

00:05:43.230 --> 00:05:46.740
The same thing in the financial
world, where you really

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:49.680
don't want to go change
the financial metrics

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:53.430
without understanding what went
on for 20 years internally.

00:05:53.430 --> 00:05:54.900
Very, very similar things here.

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:57.840
The only difference is the
consequences of doing something

00:05:57.840 --> 00:05:59.970
wrong can be catastrophic.

00:05:59.970 --> 00:06:03.454
Imagine we have to decide,
based on a model's prediction,

00:06:03.454 --> 00:06:05.370
whether they have to
shut down an offshore oil

00:06:05.370 --> 00:06:09.730
rig, which is producing about
2 million barrels a day.

00:06:09.730 --> 00:06:12.540
How to predict whether
a particular engine

00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:15.660
or a particular aircraft needs
to be pulled for maintenance

00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:17.700
this take, before
this takeoff, or is it

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:20.480
OK to leave the airport today?

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:23.800
Consequences of a wrong
decision are catastrophic.

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:26.220
And false negatives
are really not OK.

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:27.940
So there are no models
that we generally

00:06:27.940 --> 00:06:30.960
tend to build were
false negatives are OK.

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:33.400
Our false positive rate-- so
that's why generally high,

00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:34.810
roughly around 20% to 30%--

00:06:34.810 --> 00:06:38.140
would be OK for a model
in the industrial world.

00:06:38.140 --> 00:06:41.140
But a false negative
rate, meaning

00:06:41.140 --> 00:06:45.130
the risk of me saying something
is OK when it's really not OK,

00:06:45.130 --> 00:06:48.320
has to be zero, or as
close to zero as possible.

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:50.080
That's where the
consequences come in.

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:52.000
And analytic silos
exist because of that,

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:54.010
because it takes a lot
of expertise for somebody

00:06:54.010 --> 00:06:56.470
to build and maintain
these models.

00:06:56.470 --> 00:06:59.290
Now, for us to come
in and scale this,

00:06:59.290 --> 00:07:00.910
if I have to
democratize analytics

00:07:00.910 --> 00:07:04.060
for 5,000 engineers in a
company like Baker Hughes,

00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:06.100
for example, I can't
necessarily go in

00:07:06.100 --> 00:07:08.440
and say, drop
everything you're doing.

00:07:08.440 --> 00:07:10.090
Here's the brand new framework.

00:07:10.090 --> 00:07:12.670
Go build all your model to
the brand new framework.

00:07:12.670 --> 00:07:14.500
Some would, but most
of them would still

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:17.090
go back to Excel spreadsheets
and, in some cases, therefore

00:07:17.090 --> 00:07:18.050
[INAUDIBLE].

00:07:18.050 --> 00:07:20.990
But that's what works,
and they know it works.

00:07:20.990 --> 00:07:24.860
So we need to somehow
bridge these silos together,

00:07:24.860 --> 00:07:27.310
both the data silos
and analytic silos.

00:07:27.310 --> 00:07:30.972
And fundamentally, getting a
federated orchestration engine,

00:07:30.972 --> 00:07:33.430
where you have a lot of these
models talking to each other,

00:07:33.430 --> 00:07:35.980
not necessarily independent
islands, but actually

00:07:35.980 --> 00:07:38.285
models that talk to each
other is fundamental

00:07:38.285 --> 00:07:40.410
if you want to scale
analytics in industrial world.

00:07:40.410 --> 00:07:42.115
And I'll show you
some examples of that.

00:07:42.115 --> 00:07:43.600
The example that
you see here where

00:07:43.600 --> 00:07:48.580
there is a Python model that
gives an output that gets

00:07:48.580 --> 00:07:52.390
fed into a Fortran 77 model,
which we can only probably

00:07:52.390 --> 00:07:56.050
run as a black box, that
then talks to a model that's

00:07:56.050 --> 00:07:59.170
written in Go, that then talks
to a model that's written in C,

00:07:59.170 --> 00:08:00.610
it's a daily reality for us.

00:08:00.610 --> 00:08:02.900
It's not an esoteric example.

00:08:02.900 --> 00:08:04.720
In fact, there are
very, very few examples

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:08.140
where we had to build a model
that gets you to an outcome.

00:08:08.140 --> 00:08:09.868
That's just it in one framework.

00:08:12.860 --> 00:08:15.350
Now I've listed some
of these things.

00:08:15.350 --> 00:08:17.100
Some of these things
could be provocative.

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:18.860
Some of these
things you could say

00:08:18.860 --> 00:08:22.710
are myths or reality,
depending on your point of view

00:08:22.710 --> 00:08:24.461
and what your
experiences have been.

00:08:24.461 --> 00:08:25.960
But we've heard all
of these things.

00:08:25.960 --> 00:08:28.230
And we talk to customers
on a daily basis.

00:08:28.230 --> 00:08:31.260
And in respect to where
they are in their journey

00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:32.409
for digitalization.

00:08:32.409 --> 00:08:34.200
Where they are in their
journey from moving

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:36.360
to the Cloud or not.

00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:39.840
Whether they believe in AI, or
whether they think it's a myth.

00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:43.309
A lot of these things are
what customers tell us daily.

00:08:43.309 --> 00:08:48.260
Now, there is some truth to
a lot of these statements,

00:08:48.260 --> 00:08:50.700
but they hide the
bigger truth, right?

00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:52.980
So for example,
one of my favorites

00:08:52.980 --> 00:08:55.424
is when people say neural
networks are old news.

00:08:55.424 --> 00:08:57.840
The general statement is, I've
done this in the '90s, when

00:08:57.840 --> 00:08:59.330
I was doing my masters.

00:08:59.330 --> 00:09:00.570
They're really bad.

00:09:00.570 --> 00:09:03.062
They got, what,
about 60% accuracy.

00:09:03.062 --> 00:09:04.770
They really never got
anything meaningful

00:09:04.770 --> 00:09:06.930
in engineering parlance,
so we moved on.

00:09:06.930 --> 00:09:09.060
What's so new about all
these new neural networks

00:09:09.060 --> 00:09:10.087
that are coming up?

00:09:10.087 --> 00:09:11.670
And the other thing
I didn't list here

00:09:11.670 --> 00:09:16.540
was people saying, oh, I've
done deep learning 20 years ago.

00:09:16.540 --> 00:09:18.770
And for those of you who
do deep learning here,

00:09:18.770 --> 00:09:21.037
you know, that's really
a silly statement.

00:09:24.250 --> 00:09:27.150
But the reason why people
walk around with these myths

00:09:27.150 --> 00:09:29.220
is because they haven't
gotten the outcomes

00:09:29.220 --> 00:09:31.740
that everybody seems to be
promising with analytics.

00:09:31.740 --> 00:09:33.330
And by the way, the
analytics promise

00:09:33.330 --> 00:09:35.842
has been the same for
the last 25 years.

00:09:35.842 --> 00:09:37.800
That used to be the
digital well of the future,

00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:42.540
and that actually came out in
the late '80s and early '90s,

00:09:42.540 --> 00:09:45.480
But they wanted to go sensor
up the entire oilfield and say,

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:47.490
this is going to create
a huge revolution.

00:09:47.490 --> 00:09:49.640
That's like me coming
and telling you,

00:09:49.640 --> 00:09:53.950
email is going to make
you more productive.

00:09:53.950 --> 00:09:58.300
So it just has to be balanced
with what, and how, you're

00:09:58.300 --> 00:09:59.760
actually applying things to.

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:01.600
And just to bring
home the point,

00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:04.090
I wanted to walk you
through a few examples.

00:10:04.090 --> 00:10:06.750
And one of the favorite things--
and just full disclosure,

00:10:06.750 --> 00:10:07.250
right?

00:10:07.250 --> 00:10:11.030
So my background is, through and
through, aerospace engineering.

00:10:11.030 --> 00:10:12.580
I grew up in the physics world.

00:10:12.580 --> 00:10:14.590
We did data science
as a necessity.

00:10:14.590 --> 00:10:17.800
We got into doing large-scale
data science because physics

00:10:17.800 --> 00:10:19.930
alone was not sufficient.

00:10:19.930 --> 00:10:23.470
But the general pushback from
more subject matter experts

00:10:23.470 --> 00:10:26.330
is the world has been run from
[? biophysics. ?] We understand

00:10:26.330 --> 00:10:26.830
it.

00:10:26.830 --> 00:10:29.530
That's the only thing
that can solve all kinds

00:10:29.530 --> 00:10:31.550
of complicated problems, right?

00:10:31.550 --> 00:10:34.090
So if you were to
predict, purely

00:10:34.090 --> 00:10:37.230
from data, where a
particular stone would

00:10:37.230 --> 00:10:39.810
land if you threw it, and
you don't know anything

00:10:39.810 --> 00:10:42.260
about gravity, the only
thing you need to go by

00:10:42.260 --> 00:10:46.100
is observations of each of the
stones that are being thrown.

00:10:46.100 --> 00:10:48.237
If you had only purely
built that model,

00:10:48.237 --> 00:10:49.320
this is how it looks like.

00:10:55.320 --> 00:10:56.730
So the projectile is going.

00:10:56.730 --> 00:10:58.920
We don't know anything
more than saying

00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:00.420
it's just a linear projection.

00:11:00.420 --> 00:11:02.220
If this is the first
time I'm observing,

00:11:02.220 --> 00:11:06.180
it takes me more than
10,000 observations to track

00:11:06.180 --> 00:11:08.080
that the stone is even
going to come down.

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:10.450
I don't know anything
about gravity.

00:11:10.450 --> 00:11:13.020
And even the simplest model,
the most sophisticated model

00:11:13.020 --> 00:11:16.110
you can build, requires
a lot of observations.

00:11:16.110 --> 00:11:19.752
On the flip side, if you're
a high school student

00:11:19.752 --> 00:11:21.210
and you're given
the same problem--

00:11:26.578 --> 00:11:27.720
see here, stopped working--

00:11:30.470 --> 00:11:33.230
you will tell me that all the
data you need is just one data

00:11:33.230 --> 00:11:34.410
point.

00:11:34.410 --> 00:11:38.340
All you need is the
velocity, the speed,

00:11:38.340 --> 00:11:40.680
and the angle at which
you threw the stone at.

00:11:40.680 --> 00:11:43.230
Anywhere in the universe,
where there's gravity,

00:11:43.230 --> 00:11:47.050
you can predict precisely
where the stone will land.

00:11:47.050 --> 00:11:47.550
Right?

00:11:47.550 --> 00:11:51.260
Even middle school physics
will tell you that.

00:11:51.260 --> 00:11:55.260
The problem really is reality.

00:11:55.260 --> 00:11:59.360
If I didn't know exactly what
angle I threw the stone at,

00:11:59.360 --> 00:12:02.660
I just added 1% uncertainty
in initial angle,

00:12:02.660 --> 00:12:06.760
my output changes
were about 20%.

00:12:06.760 --> 00:12:10.030
If I had a 5% uncertainty in
wind speed, all of a sudden you

00:12:10.030 --> 00:12:12.850
have 100% uncertainty
where the stone will land.

00:12:12.850 --> 00:12:15.850
And this is the simplest
physics problem you can tackle.

00:12:15.850 --> 00:12:18.530
It's just one variable
and just very,

00:12:18.530 --> 00:12:19.930
very little uncertainties.

00:12:19.930 --> 00:12:21.400
Imagine if you
have to go predict

00:12:21.400 --> 00:12:23.608
whether a particular engine
is going to have a fault,

00:12:23.608 --> 00:12:26.200
or have to shut down
an offshore oil rig.

00:12:26.200 --> 00:12:28.210
Massive problem,
thousands of variables,

00:12:28.210 --> 00:12:30.830
lots of uncertainties, lots
of things you don't know.

00:12:30.830 --> 00:12:32.410
You still have to
make a decision.

00:12:32.410 --> 00:12:35.300
So physics, alone, can get
disastrous significantly.

00:12:35.300 --> 00:12:37.930
In fact, in this problem, if
we add a few more uncertainties

00:12:37.930 --> 00:12:41.100
it'll tell you the stone
is flying backwards.

00:12:41.100 --> 00:12:43.590
So we have to be very
careful with that.

00:12:43.590 --> 00:12:45.630
And the other thing
that people generally

00:12:45.630 --> 00:12:48.670
don't like to talk about is the
fact that yes, data is cool.

00:12:48.670 --> 00:12:49.950
It's everywhere.

00:12:49.950 --> 00:12:51.780
Everybody likes to use it.

00:12:51.780 --> 00:12:55.100
But the reality is not
much of it is useful.

00:12:55.100 --> 00:12:57.530
So this is a real use
case where the data

00:12:57.530 --> 00:13:00.100
set is from an actual asset.

00:13:00.100 --> 00:13:02.350
Think of this as a
very large scale asset.

00:13:02.350 --> 00:13:03.520
It could be a jet engine.

00:13:03.520 --> 00:13:04.603
It could be a gas turbine.

00:13:04.603 --> 00:13:07.310
It could be any of
those kinds of things.

00:13:07.310 --> 00:13:08.810
We are the equipment
manufacturer.

00:13:08.810 --> 00:13:11.300
We, meaning GE, [INAUDIBLE] GE.

00:13:11.300 --> 00:13:14.810
We have all of the data sets
about how it was operated.

00:13:14.810 --> 00:13:17.540
So the top left gives you
the operating condition,

00:13:17.540 --> 00:13:19.280
one of the variables
that are being

00:13:19.280 --> 00:13:23.830
measured for the entirety of
this particular assets life.

00:13:23.830 --> 00:13:26.500
The actual data there
is in blue dots.

00:13:26.500 --> 00:13:31.420
Everything else there is what
we estimated, or generated

00:13:31.420 --> 00:13:32.560
through other sources.

00:13:32.560 --> 00:13:35.890
The reason we had to do that was
because here in our databases,

00:13:35.890 --> 00:13:40.620
systems of record, it says
the asset started operating

00:13:40.620 --> 00:13:43.300
in 2001.

00:13:43.300 --> 00:13:45.060
That's their
operating condition.

00:13:45.060 --> 00:13:48.654
But it actually started
operating in 1994.

00:13:48.654 --> 00:13:50.320
And there's lots of
things that happened

00:13:50.320 --> 00:13:55.350
to it before our systems of
record actually caught up.

00:13:55.350 --> 00:13:59.980
And if I had to go predict
anything about the system,

00:13:59.980 --> 00:14:03.820
and I say, I only
go by the blue dots,

00:14:03.820 --> 00:14:07.030
it doesn't matter how
sophisticated my techniques

00:14:07.030 --> 00:14:10.510
are, how sophisticated my
data scientists are, or even

00:14:10.510 --> 00:14:13.000
the physics-based
modelers are, the models

00:14:13.000 --> 00:14:18.830
would still be wrong because
data is only about 60% correct.

00:14:18.830 --> 00:14:20.540
So we had to go correct for it.

00:14:20.540 --> 00:14:23.360
And while we started digging
into this over the last decade

00:14:23.360 --> 00:14:24.830
or so, we realized
it's not just--

00:14:24.830 --> 00:14:26.930
missing data is
all not made equal.

00:14:26.930 --> 00:14:29.960
We actually had to go invent
different ways of categorizing

00:14:29.960 --> 00:14:31.097
missing data.

00:14:31.097 --> 00:14:32.930
So that's why you have
partially missing one

00:14:32.930 --> 00:14:35.700
to-- fully missing one, two,
three, and things like that.

00:14:35.700 --> 00:14:38.460
And each one has to be estimated
using different techniques.

00:14:38.460 --> 00:14:41.360
So I imagine the data
scale we started with.

00:14:41.360 --> 00:14:43.530
That includes the missing data.

00:14:43.530 --> 00:14:46.220
If we add on the augmentation
activity that goes on

00:14:46.220 --> 00:14:49.310
to add the missing data
back in, that problem

00:14:49.310 --> 00:14:53.280
becomes two or three times
worse in terms of volumes.

00:14:53.280 --> 00:14:55.180
Because the minute
you go add data in,

00:14:55.180 --> 00:14:57.850
and if I change the color
coding to all be just blue,

00:14:57.850 --> 00:15:00.710
it would be very hard for you
to tell which was estimated

00:15:00.710 --> 00:15:02.290
and which was real.

00:15:02.290 --> 00:15:06.830
And you've got to figure out
which models would use which

00:15:06.830 --> 00:15:08.920
version of the data
sets that you're using,

00:15:08.920 --> 00:15:10.770
and how do you keep
them all in sync?

00:15:10.770 --> 00:15:12.440
That becomes a massive
problem as well.

00:15:12.440 --> 00:15:14.990
And just to show you the quality
of how well we can actually

00:15:14.990 --> 00:15:18.050
estimate, the top right
figure, blue histogram,

00:15:18.050 --> 00:15:19.800
tells you the actual data set.

00:15:19.800 --> 00:15:25.200
The mean is around 559 with a
standard deviation of about 33.

00:15:25.200 --> 00:15:31.420
The estimated values are very,
very close, 560 versus 31.

00:15:31.420 --> 00:15:34.230
So it's very hard for
someone to tell after a while

00:15:34.230 --> 00:15:36.810
whether the data set was real
or not if you didn't properly

00:15:36.810 --> 00:15:38.200
tag them.

00:15:38.200 --> 00:15:39.850
And this happens regularly.

00:15:39.850 --> 00:15:41.637
This happens on
an engineer's desk

00:15:41.637 --> 00:15:43.970
where they will change something
in the data set, passed

00:15:43.970 --> 00:15:45.690
on the data set
to somebody else,

00:15:45.690 --> 00:15:48.420
they built a model that had
a significant consequence

00:15:48.420 --> 00:15:50.240
and they pass it off.

00:15:50.240 --> 00:15:53.060
And there's no systems of
record for any of this.

00:15:53.060 --> 00:15:55.450
And that becomes the
actual data of record

00:15:55.450 --> 00:15:57.310
as well moving forward.

00:15:57.310 --> 00:16:00.970
So very quickly, industrial
analytics, for us,

00:16:00.970 --> 00:16:03.850
is a combination
of three things.

00:16:03.850 --> 00:16:06.670
People have been doing analytics
for a long time combining

00:16:06.670 --> 00:16:10.030
domain knowledge with
standard software techniques.

00:16:10.030 --> 00:16:13.000
The new kids on the block are a
new way of doing data science,

00:16:13.000 --> 00:16:15.940
mainly deep learning based
data science, and new way

00:16:15.940 --> 00:16:17.567
of building software
on the cloud micro

00:16:17.567 --> 00:16:20.265
services self-scalable.

00:16:20.265 --> 00:16:22.890
The traditional solutions are a
combination of domain knowledge

00:16:22.890 --> 00:16:24.350
with software.

00:16:24.350 --> 00:16:27.045
I would say finding
very, very hard problems

00:16:27.045 --> 00:16:28.770
or finding solutions
to hard problems,

00:16:28.770 --> 00:16:30.470
like finding a
needle in a haystack,

00:16:30.470 --> 00:16:33.690
is a combination of modern data
science with modern software.

00:16:33.690 --> 00:16:37.080
Predicting what I'm going to
buy before I actually go and buy

00:16:37.080 --> 00:16:38.670
it, hard problem.

00:16:38.670 --> 00:16:41.084
Predicting how long it will
take me to go home before

00:16:41.084 --> 00:16:42.750
I actually tell you
when I'm going to go

00:16:42.750 --> 00:16:44.600
home, again, a hard problem.

00:16:44.600 --> 00:16:48.000
In a lot of cases,
it's been solved.

00:16:48.000 --> 00:16:50.890
A lot of cases, it's
already happening today.

00:16:50.890 --> 00:16:52.920
The case in point for
industrial analytics

00:16:52.920 --> 00:16:55.140
it's really a combination
of all three things.

00:16:55.140 --> 00:16:57.348
Because it's not even finding
a needle in a haystack,

00:16:57.348 --> 00:16:59.420
it's finding a needle
in an ocean of data.

00:16:59.420 --> 00:17:01.810
Because most of
the times, the one

00:17:01.810 --> 00:17:03.435
thing that you're
trying to go predict,

00:17:03.435 --> 00:17:05.304
you don't have any data for.

00:17:05.304 --> 00:17:07.095
So if you're trying to
predict for example,

00:17:07.095 --> 00:17:09.380
a [INAUDIBLE] with
a batting fail,

00:17:09.380 --> 00:17:12.440
you would have hundreds of
sensors on a gas turbine,

00:17:12.440 --> 00:17:15.880
but you don't have a sensor on
a batting that's about to fail.

00:17:15.880 --> 00:17:17.920
Many times, the
sensors that you have

00:17:17.920 --> 00:17:22.030
are all confounded and
not necessarily calibrated

00:17:22.030 --> 00:17:23.839
in the last 20 years.

00:17:23.839 --> 00:17:26.290
So how exactly do
you make sense of it?

00:17:26.290 --> 00:17:28.540
To predict something
that you're going

00:17:28.540 --> 00:17:30.930
to go to predict without
understanding the sensors

00:17:30.930 --> 00:17:32.910
around that particular piece?

00:17:32.910 --> 00:17:35.440
That's really the problem that
the industrial world faces.

00:17:35.440 --> 00:17:37.950
And that is not just one
esoteric problem, not just

00:17:37.950 --> 00:17:39.420
one problem here [INAUDIBLE].

00:17:39.420 --> 00:17:42.450
Almost every problem of
value is a combination

00:17:42.450 --> 00:17:44.000
of all three things.

00:17:44.000 --> 00:17:46.867
And we got here, not as an
epiphany moment where we just

00:17:46.867 --> 00:17:48.450
sat there and said
this is the hardest

00:17:48.450 --> 00:17:49.810
problem we need to solve.

00:17:49.810 --> 00:17:52.170
These are the problems we
have to solve every day.

00:17:52.170 --> 00:17:53.670
These are the
problems our customers

00:17:53.670 --> 00:17:55.260
have to solve every day.

00:17:55.260 --> 00:17:57.960
If we could solve it with
traditional techniques

00:17:57.960 --> 00:17:59.022
we would.

00:17:59.022 --> 00:18:01.230
If we could solve with just
purely with data science,

00:18:01.230 --> 00:18:01.740
we would.

00:18:01.740 --> 00:18:03.300
Because that's the
easiest thing to do.

00:18:03.300 --> 00:18:04.350
There's a lot of
techniques out there.

00:18:04.350 --> 00:18:05.849
There are a lot of
systems out there

00:18:05.849 --> 00:18:07.420
that'll let you go do that.

00:18:07.420 --> 00:18:10.440
The reason we had to go combine
all three things painstakingly,

00:18:10.440 --> 00:18:12.565
because that's the only
thing that actually gets us

00:18:12.565 --> 00:18:15.120
to a solution that's meaningful.

00:18:15.120 --> 00:18:19.050
Now we talk about
digital twins a lot.

00:18:19.050 --> 00:18:21.510
And the world talks
about digital twins a lot

00:18:21.510 --> 00:18:24.840
today to the extent that
it's almost an abused term.

00:18:24.840 --> 00:18:26.770
For us, digital twins
are very special.

00:18:26.770 --> 00:18:29.730
We get there because we
can't solve these problems

00:18:29.730 --> 00:18:32.430
with any of these individual
techniques by themselves.

00:18:32.430 --> 00:18:35.460
And the way we define it is
it's a digital representation

00:18:35.460 --> 00:18:38.990
of a physical system on
an asset or a process

00:18:38.990 --> 00:18:40.780
that has a few characteristics.

00:18:40.780 --> 00:18:44.260
It has to be live, it has to
be continuously update-able,

00:18:44.260 --> 00:18:47.850
and it has to be adaptable, and
scalable all at the same time.

00:18:47.850 --> 00:18:49.920
Now, imagine I have to
do something specific,

00:18:49.920 --> 00:18:52.880
or I have to make something
specific and scalable

00:18:52.880 --> 00:18:53.690
at the same time.

00:18:53.690 --> 00:18:56.540
Meaning, I go build a
model of a particular asset

00:18:56.540 --> 00:18:58.234
or a particular system.

00:18:58.234 --> 00:18:59.900
I make it so specific
that it's specific

00:18:59.900 --> 00:19:01.310
to that particular system.

00:19:01.310 --> 00:19:03.200
It doesn't scale by definition.

00:19:03.200 --> 00:19:05.450
So how do you go build
something that's both specific,

00:19:05.450 --> 00:19:06.930
and scalable?

00:19:06.930 --> 00:19:08.580
Even worse, I made it specific.

00:19:08.580 --> 00:19:10.890
OK, I can somehow
make it scalable.

00:19:10.890 --> 00:19:12.635
I now have to go
make it adaptable.

00:19:12.635 --> 00:19:14.010
Meaning, if I go
change something

00:19:14.010 --> 00:19:16.560
in the physical system,
I change a pump out,

00:19:16.560 --> 00:19:18.630
I change a compressor
out, I should

00:19:18.630 --> 00:19:20.340
be able to change
only that portion

00:19:20.340 --> 00:19:22.440
of my entire digital twin
and make the whole thing

00:19:22.440 --> 00:19:23.190
work again.

00:19:23.190 --> 00:19:25.776
I don't have to go back and
rebuild the whole thing.

00:19:25.776 --> 00:19:27.400
Those are very, very
hard things to do.

00:19:27.400 --> 00:19:28.920
And the reason we
have to go do it,

00:19:28.920 --> 00:19:31.380
is because we have to run
digital twins thousands

00:19:31.380 --> 00:19:33.810
of times a day for every
single implementation we

00:19:33.810 --> 00:19:34.950
have out there.

00:19:34.950 --> 00:19:37.980
So it's hundreds of thousands
of digital twins running

00:19:37.980 --> 00:19:39.910
thousands of times a day.

00:19:39.910 --> 00:19:41.520
So we can't really
run these at scale

00:19:41.520 --> 00:19:43.470
if we have to go do
these things manually.

00:19:43.470 --> 00:19:45.090
Typically, how these
things are done,

00:19:45.090 --> 00:19:49.530
you get a team of six to 10
PSTs with some designers who

00:19:49.530 --> 00:19:51.600
are experts in the
system, let them loose

00:19:51.600 --> 00:19:53.430
for about six months,
and they come up

00:19:53.430 --> 00:19:57.100
with one model that's specific,
but not scalable and not

00:19:57.100 --> 00:19:58.704
adaptable.

00:19:58.704 --> 00:20:00.870
And the reason why we have
to go mix all these three

00:20:00.870 --> 00:20:03.295
things together, so we
always start with physics--

00:20:03.295 --> 00:20:05.670
that's physics-based so that
we can actually build on top

00:20:05.670 --> 00:20:06.660
of it--

00:20:06.660 --> 00:20:09.857
where physics allows
us to do known knowns.

00:20:09.857 --> 00:20:11.940
So if you know something
is going to be a problem,

00:20:11.940 --> 00:20:15.670
physics will get you to a
precise solution very quickly.

00:20:15.670 --> 00:20:18.260
Probablistics, because
that's where we started with,

00:20:18.260 --> 00:20:21.450
with our uncertainties I really
cannot predict the real world.

00:20:21.450 --> 00:20:24.690
Deep learning allows us to
really sleep reasonably well

00:20:24.690 --> 00:20:27.690
at night because it
allows us to go and deploy

00:20:27.690 --> 00:20:30.030
these systems to
watch for things

00:20:30.030 --> 00:20:31.570
that I know are
going to go wrong,

00:20:31.570 --> 00:20:33.777
but I don't know what they are.

00:20:33.777 --> 00:20:35.360
I know something is
going to go wrong.

00:20:35.360 --> 00:20:36.719
That's all I know.

00:20:36.719 --> 00:20:37.510
I don't know where.

00:20:37.510 --> 00:20:38.810
I don't know when.

00:20:38.810 --> 00:20:42.550
So those systems allow us to
go watch and adapt the models

00:20:42.550 --> 00:20:43.699
for that.

00:20:43.699 --> 00:20:45.365
So I'll give you a
few examples of that.

00:20:45.365 --> 00:20:47.680
I'll make it a little
concrete for you

00:20:47.680 --> 00:20:49.810
for what a digital twin is.

00:20:49.810 --> 00:20:51.310
If somebody comes
and tells you they

00:20:51.310 --> 00:20:52.755
have a digital twin of a well.

00:20:52.755 --> 00:20:54.130
And in this
particular case, this

00:20:54.130 --> 00:20:56.360
is a well with an
artificial lift system.

00:20:56.360 --> 00:20:57.640
Think of that as a pump.

00:20:57.640 --> 00:21:00.460
Its an electric submersible
pump that's about two miles deep

00:21:00.460 --> 00:21:02.480
under the earth.

00:21:02.480 --> 00:21:04.300
When we say its a
digital twin of a well,

00:21:04.300 --> 00:21:06.100
we connect it as a water model--

00:21:06.100 --> 00:21:07.630
that's the IPR curve--

00:21:07.630 --> 00:21:11.050
to a pipe model,
to a pump model,

00:21:11.050 --> 00:21:13.030
to a tubing model
that finally gets you

00:21:13.030 --> 00:21:14.675
a pressure differentiable
at the end.

00:21:14.675 --> 00:21:16.050
And what you're
trying to predict

00:21:16.050 --> 00:21:18.040
is how do I change the
operating conditions

00:21:18.040 --> 00:21:21.670
of the pump and the well to
make sure that I produce as much

00:21:21.670 --> 00:21:23.730
as possible without
damaging anything,

00:21:23.730 --> 00:21:26.410
both the well as
well as the pump.

00:21:26.410 --> 00:21:29.260
And to do this, roughly
one digital twin

00:21:29.260 --> 00:21:32.620
has about eight models
running in sequence

00:21:32.620 --> 00:21:34.910
along with an iterative
loop internally.

00:21:34.910 --> 00:21:37.560
So it's not just
a linear sequence.

00:21:37.560 --> 00:21:40.710
And this, we would consider
a fairly moderate level

00:21:40.710 --> 00:21:42.570
of complexity digital twin.

00:21:42.570 --> 00:21:46.550
Now the way we use it is an
operator would go in and say,

00:21:46.550 --> 00:21:49.330
OK, how do I improve
my production

00:21:49.330 --> 00:21:51.070
from my set of wells?

00:21:51.070 --> 00:21:53.110
To answer that
question on one well,

00:21:53.110 --> 00:21:55.510
I typically have to
run a digital twin

00:21:55.510 --> 00:21:57.490
at least a few thousand times.

00:21:57.490 --> 00:21:58.960
And to get a plot
like this, where

00:21:58.960 --> 00:22:02.350
I have a plot of power
versus total liquid flow

00:22:02.350 --> 00:22:05.560
from that particular well by
changing all the parameters,

00:22:05.560 --> 00:22:07.690
all the gray dots, each
one of those gray dots

00:22:07.690 --> 00:22:11.500
is a prediction, or an inference
through the digital twin.

00:22:11.500 --> 00:22:15.740
The best family of solutions
are on the blue dots.

00:22:15.740 --> 00:22:20.530
The problem is, today I might be
operating on the black diamond.

00:22:20.530 --> 00:22:24.730
Without knowing what my space
looks like, I might just

00:22:24.730 --> 00:22:27.520
randomly increase power and
hit any one of those red dots

00:22:27.520 --> 00:22:30.760
on the top where I
increase power, meaning I'm

00:22:30.760 --> 00:22:34.760
spending more, and I'm
actually producing less.

00:22:34.760 --> 00:22:37.670
This happens a lot
where intuitively you

00:22:37.670 --> 00:22:39.264
can go tweak some
things, you really

00:22:39.264 --> 00:22:41.180
don't know where you are
on your design space.

00:22:41.180 --> 00:22:42.971
In this case, it's a
fairly simple problem.

00:22:42.971 --> 00:22:45.110
We're only changing
about six parameters.

00:22:45.110 --> 00:22:47.750
But if you're in an oil field,
there are a few hundred wells,

00:22:47.750 --> 00:22:50.720
you're automatically going
into a few thousand parameter

00:22:50.720 --> 00:22:52.520
optimization problem
very quickly.

00:22:52.520 --> 00:22:56.570
And that needs to happen
a couple of times a day.

00:22:56.570 --> 00:22:59.660
But today, the best you
can do with the tools

00:22:59.660 --> 00:23:01.520
out there, not just
in oil and gas,

00:23:01.520 --> 00:23:04.760
but in aviation and power or
any one of these industries,

00:23:04.760 --> 00:23:07.040
is to run an optimization
like this, not even

00:23:07.040 --> 00:23:09.370
a global optimizer,
but a local optimizer.

00:23:09.370 --> 00:23:12.500
It can take anywhere
from 20 minutes

00:23:12.500 --> 00:23:17.150
to about two hours for an
advanced engineer to go run it.

00:23:17.150 --> 00:23:19.580
So we took this
as an experiment.

00:23:19.580 --> 00:23:24.710
And about summer of last year we
went and built the digital twin

00:23:24.710 --> 00:23:25.790
first for a single well.

00:23:25.790 --> 00:23:27.790
It took about three
minutes per well.

00:23:27.790 --> 00:23:30.350
We scaled it with the
techniques we talked about up

00:23:30.350 --> 00:23:34.910
to now, combined physics, data
science, and software scalable

00:23:34.910 --> 00:23:36.470
on the cloud.

00:23:36.470 --> 00:23:39.920
In about six months, we
got 1,000 x improvement

00:23:39.920 --> 00:23:41.690
going from a single
well optimization that

00:23:41.690 --> 00:23:45.490
took about three minutes to
run to a cluster of wells

00:23:45.490 --> 00:23:48.470
where about 20 wells
within a cluster, you

00:23:48.470 --> 00:23:51.260
can optimize it in
about 30 seconds.

00:23:51.260 --> 00:23:53.270
The reason why you get
1,000 x improvement

00:23:53.270 --> 00:23:56.530
is because it's not a
one-shot optimization.

00:23:56.530 --> 00:23:58.010
Its a game of whack-a-mole.

00:23:58.010 --> 00:23:59.971
So you go do something
else in one well,

00:23:59.971 --> 00:24:01.720
something else goes
wrong in another well.

00:24:01.720 --> 00:24:04.250
So you fix, go somewhere
else, change something,

00:24:04.250 --> 00:24:05.530
something else goes wrong.

00:24:05.530 --> 00:24:07.280
So you do that iteratively
multiple times.

00:24:07.280 --> 00:24:08.654
In fact, to get
to that solution,

00:24:08.654 --> 00:24:11.810
you need it run the
model about 20,000 times

00:24:11.810 --> 00:24:12.980
to get to that answer.

00:24:12.980 --> 00:24:14.396
That's really where
you're getting

00:24:14.396 --> 00:24:15.490
the 1,000 x improvement.

00:24:15.490 --> 00:24:17.840
But we wanted to push it
one level higher saying, OK,

00:24:17.840 --> 00:24:19.040
we keep the models the same.

00:24:19.040 --> 00:24:20.870
We scale the software.

00:24:20.870 --> 00:24:22.124
We push it to the Cloud.

00:24:22.124 --> 00:24:23.790
We actually orchestrate
all these models

00:24:23.790 --> 00:24:26.000
so they're on the
Cloud and see what

00:24:26.000 --> 00:24:28.430
actually can be done in a
field-level optimization.

00:24:28.430 --> 00:24:30.450
The solution
doesn't exist today.

00:24:30.450 --> 00:24:33.016
And to be honest, none of our
customers even asked us for it.

00:24:33.016 --> 00:24:34.390
In fact, when we
go and presented

00:24:34.390 --> 00:24:37.410
the 20-well optimization, the
response was, this is great.

00:24:37.410 --> 00:24:39.770
Today, we do that
once every six months.

00:24:39.770 --> 00:24:43.944
This one might let us do
it once every two weeks.

00:24:43.944 --> 00:24:45.860
And the field-level
optimization, the response

00:24:45.860 --> 00:24:49.092
was, we really did not know
that this is even possible.

00:24:49.092 --> 00:24:50.800
And their response
was actually opposite.

00:24:50.800 --> 00:24:53.200
They came back and said,
can you do 5,000 wells?

00:24:53.200 --> 00:24:56.080
Can you do 9,000
wells simultaneously?

00:24:56.080 --> 00:24:58.830
Because the pace at
which this is moving,

00:24:58.830 --> 00:25:00.580
the six month deadline
was just because we

00:25:00.580 --> 00:25:01.788
had our internal conferences.

00:25:01.788 --> 00:25:04.490
We actually had it scaled
in about three months.

00:25:04.490 --> 00:25:06.690
I'll show you how we
walked through that.

00:25:06.690 --> 00:25:09.370
And in just a year,
for an engineer

00:25:09.370 --> 00:25:12.520
to go from maybe doing a
single-well optimization

00:25:12.520 --> 00:25:15.400
every six months to doing
a field-level optimization

00:25:15.400 --> 00:25:18.772
a couple of times a day is
a phenomenal improvement.

00:25:18.772 --> 00:25:20.050
[APPLAUSE]

00:25:20.050 --> 00:25:20.940
OK.

00:25:20.940 --> 00:25:26.940
And the notion of
using deep learning

00:25:26.940 --> 00:25:29.730
in traditional industries
like oil and gas,

00:25:29.730 --> 00:25:33.450
or aviation, or power,
they're very risk averse.

00:25:33.450 --> 00:25:37.630
They don't really want to
trust the model by itself.

00:25:37.630 --> 00:25:39.932
So here's some of the
things that people generally

00:25:39.932 --> 00:25:41.140
talk about anomaly detection.

00:25:41.140 --> 00:25:44.400
But the things that resonate
with industrial customers

00:25:44.400 --> 00:25:47.280
is people don't really
want to go set routes.

00:25:47.280 --> 00:25:49.170
Most of anomaly
detection systems

00:25:49.170 --> 00:25:51.930
out there want to tell
you to come and say,

00:25:51.930 --> 00:25:54.450
here is my threshold
for a set of variables.

00:25:54.450 --> 00:25:56.200
Set my thresholds,
and after that, I'll

00:25:56.200 --> 00:25:58.200
go tell you when something
is going to go wrong.

00:25:58.200 --> 00:26:00.720
And this has to be done a priori
before it goes and catches

00:26:00.720 --> 00:26:01.650
things.

00:26:01.650 --> 00:26:04.680
The biggest promise of
automated anomaly detection

00:26:04.680 --> 00:26:08.310
with deep learning primarily
is you can actually

00:26:08.310 --> 00:26:11.580
catch anomalies without
necessarily knowing what

00:26:11.580 --> 00:26:13.520
these anomalies are beforehand.

00:26:13.520 --> 00:26:15.020
I don't have to
train for anomalies.

00:26:15.020 --> 00:26:16.660
I don't have to train rules.

00:26:16.660 --> 00:26:18.630
In fact, I don't
even need to tell it

00:26:18.630 --> 00:26:20.886
what are the sets of inputs
I need to be looking at.

00:26:20.886 --> 00:26:23.010
As long as you have a data
stream coming in, and as

00:26:23.010 --> 00:26:26.500
long as you knew what the
normal state was, I can go

00:26:26.500 --> 00:26:28.910
say what was abnormal.

00:26:28.910 --> 00:26:32.002
That is the first foray
for us into deep learning.

00:26:32.002 --> 00:26:33.960
But from there, we actually
went into deploying

00:26:33.960 --> 00:26:38.130
a live application for a
customer where the customer was

00:26:38.130 --> 00:26:42.630
recording about 10,000 sensors
off an offshore oil rig.

00:26:42.630 --> 00:26:44.830
Actually, this was
done by the customer

00:26:44.830 --> 00:26:48.130
who actually already pushed
the data to Google Cloud.

00:26:48.130 --> 00:26:52.740
All we had to do was train with
a six month data set they had,

00:26:52.740 --> 00:26:55.910
which was normal,
with no abnormalities.

00:26:55.910 --> 00:27:01.095
And within about a six week
period, starting from scratch,

00:27:01.095 --> 00:27:03.470
going from not knowing what
the data is, not even knowing

00:27:03.470 --> 00:27:07.070
what the asset was, to having
an anomaly detection working

00:27:07.070 --> 00:27:10.680
in an application roughly
took us about six weeks.

00:27:10.680 --> 00:27:13.110
And this particular
application that you see here,

00:27:13.110 --> 00:27:16.710
this failure mode
that was caught,

00:27:16.710 --> 00:27:18.930
that's shown in the top
right where it's just

00:27:18.930 --> 00:27:21.090
an anomaly score,
has a combination

00:27:21.090 --> 00:27:23.970
of about 16 different
sensors-- record an anomaly.

00:27:23.970 --> 00:27:26.160
Then the next immediate
question is, OK, this

00:27:26.160 --> 00:27:29.340
is something going wrong,
but is this just a blip,

00:27:29.340 --> 00:27:32.250
or can you tell me what
is causing this problem?

00:27:32.250 --> 00:27:33.750
That's the blot on
the bottom right.

00:27:33.750 --> 00:27:36.180
And I'm not sure if it's
showing it properly here,

00:27:36.180 --> 00:27:38.850
but the colors actually
indicate as a function

00:27:38.850 --> 00:27:41.610
of time, which
variables are actually

00:27:41.610 --> 00:27:43.571
affecting the anomaly score.

00:27:43.571 --> 00:27:45.570
So you can actually start
going and figuring out

00:27:45.570 --> 00:27:47.010
what is causing the anomaly.

00:27:47.010 --> 00:27:48.140
That's the next step.

00:27:48.140 --> 00:27:49.980
The step after that is
actually figuring out

00:27:49.980 --> 00:27:53.076
what is the actual cause, what
is the root cause, actually.

00:27:53.076 --> 00:27:55.200
And to do that, you actually
train with maintenance

00:27:55.200 --> 00:27:58.450
logs along with this time
series data sets as well.

00:27:58.450 --> 00:28:00.270
So this is actually
an autoencoder

00:28:00.270 --> 00:28:05.680
running for a set of about
24 different variables

00:28:05.680 --> 00:28:08.150
over a period of two years.

00:28:08.150 --> 00:28:11.490
Built, running, live on
TCP in about six weeks.

00:28:14.490 --> 00:28:17.130
The other kind of
systems that are actually

00:28:17.130 --> 00:28:20.490
fairly effective for
using in deep learning

00:28:20.490 --> 00:28:24.640
is image recognition
kind of use cases.

00:28:24.640 --> 00:28:27.840
So this is a use case where if
you're driven from here to LA,

00:28:27.840 --> 00:28:29.730
you would have seen
a lot of these pumps.

00:28:29.730 --> 00:28:31.590
These are called rod lift pumps.

00:28:31.590 --> 00:28:34.740
And the biggest problem
with rod lift pumps

00:28:34.740 --> 00:28:36.930
are they fail quite often.

00:28:36.930 --> 00:28:38.940
And their failure
modes generally

00:28:38.940 --> 00:28:41.160
are categorized using
these images, which

00:28:41.160 --> 00:28:45.090
is a plot of the force where's
a displacement of the rod that

00:28:45.090 --> 00:28:47.280
keeps going in and out.

00:28:47.280 --> 00:28:51.030
And depending on how
that shape looks,

00:28:51.030 --> 00:28:54.300
an experienced
engineer can say what

00:28:54.300 --> 00:28:56.470
is the actual failure mode.

00:28:56.470 --> 00:29:00.700
The problem is two failure modes
look more or less the same.

00:29:00.700 --> 00:29:03.060
So it depends on
who you ask and when

00:29:03.060 --> 00:29:06.810
you ask in terms of finding
out what exactly is available,

00:29:06.810 --> 00:29:10.590
until you actually have to stop
operations and pull it back up.

00:29:10.590 --> 00:29:13.530
And whereas if you use
traditional techniques to do

00:29:13.530 --> 00:29:18.030
distance-based metrics to say,
this is the overall graph.

00:29:18.030 --> 00:29:20.110
Tell me what this
new graph looks like,

00:29:20.110 --> 00:29:21.720
and what is it
closest to, you get

00:29:21.720 --> 00:29:24.640
to an accuracy of about 60%.

00:29:24.640 --> 00:29:30.560
So we took the latest test and
image object recognition model,

00:29:30.560 --> 00:29:34.040
augmented it using transfer
learning with just the failure

00:29:34.040 --> 00:29:37.090
mode images that we
had and let it loose.

00:29:37.090 --> 00:29:42.330
So if I run this
demo for you, you

00:29:42.330 --> 00:29:45.389
can see most of the detections
where on the left-hand side

00:29:45.389 --> 00:29:47.680
is the data set that's coming
in for a particular pump,

00:29:47.680 --> 00:29:49.050
for a particular model of pump.

00:29:49.050 --> 00:29:50.790
On the right-hand
side is what the model

00:29:50.790 --> 00:29:53.450
is predicting real-time.

00:29:53.450 --> 00:29:56.420
So this is a deep learning
model built and deployed

00:29:56.420 --> 00:29:58.910
for inference on, in
this particular case,

00:29:58.910 --> 00:30:02.930
the times are actually
running on an edge device

00:30:02.930 --> 00:30:07.050
with a small [? Jetson ?]
with the timing

00:30:07.050 --> 00:30:08.580
that we're actually running.

00:30:08.580 --> 00:30:11.790
So you can see
instantly it is catching

00:30:11.790 --> 00:30:16.490
with under one second delay
what the actual failure mode is.

00:30:16.490 --> 00:30:19.280
And this model was really
not that optimized yet.

00:30:19.280 --> 00:30:22.760
We just took the standard model
that was published, augmented

00:30:22.760 --> 00:30:25.220
it with the data sets
we had, and accuracy

00:30:25.220 --> 00:30:27.680
went from about 65% to 93%.

00:30:30.620 --> 00:30:32.410
The data sets were
not even that high.

00:30:32.410 --> 00:30:35.650
So at only a few
hundred failure modes.

00:30:35.650 --> 00:30:39.370
And if you optimize the models,
you can get up to about 93%

00:30:39.370 --> 00:30:41.117
to about 97%.

00:30:41.117 --> 00:30:42.700
The last 3% is
something that you have

00:30:42.700 --> 00:30:45.646
to go collect more data for.

00:30:45.646 --> 00:30:47.020
So these are some
of the examples

00:30:47.020 --> 00:30:50.090
that we are building and
deploying in the field,

00:30:50.090 --> 00:30:54.400
both with just traditional
deep learning, augmenting

00:30:54.400 --> 00:30:55.750
with deep learning.

00:30:55.750 --> 00:30:57.610
The other kind of
deep learning models

00:30:57.610 --> 00:30:59.230
that we are building
quite effectively

00:30:59.230 --> 00:31:01.660
are the knowledge systems.

00:31:01.660 --> 00:31:04.750
And the request for this one
came from a couple of customers

00:31:04.750 --> 00:31:10.120
where the request was, we
have a lot of documents

00:31:10.120 --> 00:31:13.420
that we have generated over
the last 30 to 50 years.

00:31:13.420 --> 00:31:15.250
We have a lot of
training material

00:31:15.250 --> 00:31:18.100
that we generally use
for our new recruits

00:31:18.100 --> 00:31:21.012
to be trained on our
operations and to make sure

00:31:21.012 --> 00:31:22.720
that we can actually
get them up to speed

00:31:22.720 --> 00:31:25.570
very quickly with our
subject matter experts.

00:31:25.570 --> 00:31:27.770
What most of the
industries are facing today

00:31:27.770 --> 00:31:29.146
are a dichotomy of problems.

00:31:29.146 --> 00:31:30.520
The first problem
is that they're

00:31:30.520 --> 00:31:34.900
losing a significant portion
of their experienced workforce

00:31:34.900 --> 00:31:37.360
in the next five to 10 years.

00:31:37.360 --> 00:31:40.580
On the other side, the new
workforce that's coming in

00:31:40.580 --> 00:31:43.540
are used to learning
and interacting

00:31:43.540 --> 00:31:44.900
in a very, very different way.

00:31:44.900 --> 00:31:46.316
They don't necessarily
want to sit

00:31:46.316 --> 00:31:49.930
through three weeks of training
on just going through and doing

00:31:49.930 --> 00:31:50.796
one task.

00:31:50.796 --> 00:31:52.170
They want to be
able to go learn.

00:31:52.170 --> 00:31:55.090
They want to be able to go
search and find information

00:31:55.090 --> 00:31:56.620
as they need it.

00:31:56.620 --> 00:31:59.650
The problem is, to
enable that and to bridge

00:31:59.650 --> 00:32:01.360
the gap between people
who are retiring

00:32:01.360 --> 00:32:02.734
and the people
who are coming in,

00:32:02.734 --> 00:32:06.850
you need a knowledge system that
can quickly learn and adapt.

00:32:06.850 --> 00:32:10.450
So in this case, we built
up the knowledge system.

00:32:10.450 --> 00:32:14.170
So we had the standard
English language NLP models.

00:32:14.170 --> 00:32:18.790
We took those and we trained
those models to understand

00:32:18.790 --> 00:32:20.230
technical English.

00:32:20.230 --> 00:32:22.630
So how do you go
interpret tables?

00:32:22.630 --> 00:32:26.200
How do you go interpret
tables and figure captions?

00:32:26.200 --> 00:32:29.080
How do you go get a
natural language query

00:32:29.080 --> 00:32:32.980
to return an answer that's not
a pure retrieval, but actually

00:32:32.980 --> 00:32:35.590
something that generates text?

00:32:35.590 --> 00:32:37.030
So we had to go build that.

00:32:37.030 --> 00:32:38.560
And after we built
that, we realized

00:32:38.560 --> 00:32:41.350
that the corpus of data that
we had to go to build it,

00:32:41.350 --> 00:32:43.930
had to be expanded with all
the subject matter expertise

00:32:43.930 --> 00:32:47.320
we had internally
in Baker Hughes, GE.

00:32:47.320 --> 00:32:49.990
so we made an
English-language-speaking model

00:32:49.990 --> 00:32:51.790
understand the
technical language

00:32:51.790 --> 00:32:54.250
based on all the technical
language documents that

00:32:54.250 --> 00:32:55.600
are out there in the world.

00:32:55.600 --> 00:32:58.360
Then we made that model
into an oil and gas person.

00:32:58.360 --> 00:33:00.760
Actually, we made that
into BH-GE person,

00:33:00.760 --> 00:33:03.012
because it understood
our language.

00:33:03.012 --> 00:33:05.470
Then we took that model and
tried to apply it to a customer

00:33:05.470 --> 00:33:08.500
and realized it was like
a BH-GE person talking

00:33:08.500 --> 00:33:12.760
to a person in BP or Shell.

00:33:12.760 --> 00:33:14.560
We can understand each
other, but then we

00:33:14.560 --> 00:33:16.270
don't know our lingo exactly.

00:33:16.270 --> 00:33:18.430
We have our own acronyms.

00:33:18.430 --> 00:33:21.790
So we had to go get the
documents from the customers

00:33:21.790 --> 00:33:24.020
to augment this model as well.

00:33:24.020 --> 00:33:26.760
So just to get to a system that
understands a natural language

00:33:26.760 --> 00:33:31.000
query, respond to it in
the customer's context,

00:33:31.000 --> 00:33:33.730
to get to, for
example, a user input,

00:33:33.730 --> 00:33:36.460
going in, having a spelling
correction that's oil and gas

00:33:36.460 --> 00:33:39.640
specific, and customer
specific, and go through

00:33:39.640 --> 00:33:44.380
and give recommendations based
on what answers could be given.

00:33:44.380 --> 00:33:46.390
Because if you go and
ask a general question,

00:33:46.390 --> 00:33:48.940
you can of course go to Google
or any of the search engines

00:33:48.940 --> 00:33:50.700
and then ask a general
question, you're

00:33:50.700 --> 00:33:53.200
going to get a very,
very generic answer.

00:33:53.200 --> 00:33:54.760
What they really
want is an answer

00:33:54.760 --> 00:33:56.320
that's specific to
the person who's

00:33:56.320 --> 00:33:59.020
asking the question in the
context of the company that's

00:33:59.020 --> 00:34:00.590
being asked.

00:34:00.590 --> 00:34:03.150
So to just get this
one simple example

00:34:03.150 --> 00:34:06.850
working where somebody went
and tried typing in "gammar,"

00:34:06.850 --> 00:34:08.739
so if you go try
that in Google today,

00:34:08.739 --> 00:34:11.170
it'll get corrected as
"grammar" or "grammarly" or any

00:34:11.170 --> 00:34:14.600
of the many English
language corrections.

00:34:14.600 --> 00:34:18.360
What they actually wanted was
a "gamma board" or a "HT gamma

00:34:18.360 --> 00:34:19.610
elite."

00:34:19.610 --> 00:34:22.550
That is specific to that
particular customer.

00:34:22.550 --> 00:34:25.130
They also wanted "grammarly"
and other things,

00:34:25.130 --> 00:34:27.590
but they would be
ranked down, much

00:34:27.590 --> 00:34:30.440
below what a general purpose
search engine would give you.

00:34:30.440 --> 00:34:32.810
Imagine extending this
out where you just

00:34:32.810 --> 00:34:36.290
go start entering natural
language queries and a report,

00:34:36.290 --> 00:34:38.810
an actual full blown
report gets generated,

00:34:38.810 --> 00:34:42.107
for you to go train yourself
for the query that you entered.

00:34:42.107 --> 00:34:43.940
That's really where the
oil and gas language

00:34:43.940 --> 00:34:45.300
model is getting used.

00:34:45.300 --> 00:34:47.690
In fact, one of
the models that we

00:34:47.690 --> 00:34:50.650
built which would require
significant compute

00:34:50.650 --> 00:34:52.130
is the oil and gas
language model.

00:34:52.130 --> 00:34:54.750
And we continue to
evolve that as we speak.

00:34:54.750 --> 00:34:56.900
And we started, of
course, with English,

00:34:56.900 --> 00:34:59.630
but because we are an
international company,

00:34:59.630 --> 00:35:03.050
and also the corpus
of understanding

00:35:03.050 --> 00:35:06.140
about any of the industrial
world is not just in English.

00:35:06.140 --> 00:35:08.420
We actually are starting
to build translators

00:35:08.420 --> 00:35:11.685
from English into all kinds
of other languages as well.

00:35:11.685 --> 00:35:12.185
OK.

00:35:16.940 --> 00:35:18.640
The other thing we
wanted to talk about

00:35:18.640 --> 00:35:21.520
was democratizing analytics.

00:35:21.520 --> 00:35:24.910
Now we talked about data silos,
we talked about analytics,

00:35:24.910 --> 00:35:27.100
or subject matter expert
silos, but there's

00:35:27.100 --> 00:35:29.440
also the other problem that
we constantly come across.

00:35:29.440 --> 00:35:33.010
Hey, this is working on
my laptop or on my system.

00:35:33.010 --> 00:35:36.440
I've shipped it to
DevOps, or a CloudOps,

00:35:36.440 --> 00:35:38.260
or another analytics person.

00:35:38.260 --> 00:35:40.530
Now it's your problem to run it.

00:35:40.530 --> 00:35:42.690
It just works fine on my system.

00:35:42.690 --> 00:35:44.910
Containers solve
that significantly.

00:35:44.910 --> 00:35:48.280
But we had to upscale or
up-level our analytics folks,

00:35:48.280 --> 00:35:51.510
because they come, not just
from software or programming

00:35:51.510 --> 00:35:54.270
backgrounds, but they come from
deep engineering, and physics,

00:35:54.270 --> 00:35:56.690
and domain expertise
backgrounds.

00:35:56.690 --> 00:35:59.780
So we just did a quick
check of how long it took us

00:35:59.780 --> 00:36:01.810
to onboard somebody.

00:36:01.810 --> 00:36:05.200
So usually, based on
a person's background,

00:36:05.200 --> 00:36:08.260
it would take anywhere from
a week to a couple of months

00:36:08.260 --> 00:36:11.730
to fully onboard somebody
to be productive in building

00:36:11.730 --> 00:36:14.800
new analytics and pushing it
into our production systems.

00:36:14.800 --> 00:36:19.660
And that was before containers,
before we had CICD pipelines,

00:36:19.660 --> 00:36:22.360
both on the Cloud or
on somebody's laptop,

00:36:22.360 --> 00:36:24.409
it was more or less
mirrored the same way.

00:36:24.409 --> 00:36:26.200
So it's not just about
upscaling, it's also

00:36:26.200 --> 00:36:27.310
downscaling.

00:36:27.310 --> 00:36:29.890
How do you downscale
your overall system

00:36:29.890 --> 00:36:32.620
so that somebody can do their
work in their small ecosystem

00:36:32.620 --> 00:36:36.070
and then push it back into
the overall systems as well?

00:36:36.070 --> 00:36:39.910
So we went from somebody taking
anywhere from a week to two

00:36:39.910 --> 00:36:43.030
months to a couple of hours.

00:36:43.030 --> 00:36:45.800
And these were all tested
on a variety of people

00:36:45.800 --> 00:36:48.940
where you had a lot of expertise
with containers and almost

00:36:48.940 --> 00:36:50.470
no expertise in containers.

00:36:50.470 --> 00:36:54.010
In fact, we had people who came
in with absolutely no expertise

00:36:54.010 --> 00:36:56.500
in containers or
programming who still

00:36:56.500 --> 00:37:00.400
got up and running in the first
day to push production code.

00:37:00.400 --> 00:37:02.450
The other one was
we went through

00:37:02.450 --> 00:37:04.510
painful quarterly releases.

00:37:04.510 --> 00:37:08.830
It used to be nerve racking
sitting right before midnight

00:37:08.830 --> 00:37:11.080
because we had already
promised our leadership that we

00:37:11.080 --> 00:37:12.520
are going to release today.

00:37:12.520 --> 00:37:15.520
And something that was working
just fine the day before,

00:37:15.520 --> 00:37:18.160
or even in the morning,
would break just 10 minutes

00:37:18.160 --> 00:37:19.480
before production.

00:37:19.480 --> 00:37:20.960
We've all been there.

00:37:20.960 --> 00:37:24.340
And it was painful, painful
quarterly releases to today,

00:37:24.340 --> 00:37:26.380
we can actually do
continuous releases

00:37:26.380 --> 00:37:28.630
from a person who has built
analytics, who does not

00:37:28.630 --> 00:37:31.690
necessarily understand the
overall working mechanics

00:37:31.690 --> 00:37:34.440
of a large-scale
enterprise software system,

00:37:34.440 --> 00:37:36.760
pushing something, making
it into production,

00:37:36.760 --> 00:37:40.430
and actually being OK
pushing it into production.

00:37:40.430 --> 00:37:42.760
And we can do that in
some specific cases.

00:37:42.760 --> 00:37:45.550
Of course, I'm not claiming
we do that in all cases.

00:37:45.550 --> 00:37:49.510
But for the capability
for the analytics person

00:37:49.510 --> 00:37:52.920
to write code, and the same
code to be shipped to production

00:37:52.920 --> 00:37:56.610
is fundamental if we
have scale any of this.

00:37:56.610 --> 00:37:59.375
Now I'll quickly run
you through a demo.

00:37:59.375 --> 00:38:00.750
The demo I'm going
to run through

00:38:00.750 --> 00:38:06.480
is trying to build a few
thousand models rather quickly

00:38:06.480 --> 00:38:07.470
on GCP.

00:38:07.470 --> 00:38:12.360
And to enable this, we built
almost all of our services

00:38:12.360 --> 00:38:15.300
using Cloud native
Google services.

00:38:15.300 --> 00:38:19.560
And we have an internal
tool that we also

00:38:19.560 --> 00:38:21.120
use as an analytics
engine for all

00:38:21.120 --> 00:38:24.160
of our products for the
customers called Applied AI.

00:38:24.160 --> 00:38:27.280
We are running Applied
AI on GCP core services.

00:38:27.280 --> 00:38:30.340
So it's our core running, but
using the GCP core services.

00:38:30.340 --> 00:38:32.730
So we are using the
Kubernetes engine.

00:38:32.730 --> 00:38:35.290
We are using, say, all
of the storage services

00:38:35.290 --> 00:38:38.260
that Google provides natively,
including all the perimeter

00:38:38.260 --> 00:38:42.200
security, DNS, Cloud DNS, and
firewall rules and everything.

00:38:42.200 --> 00:38:42.850
OK.

00:38:42.850 --> 00:38:45.820
And hopefully the demo
gods are smiling on us.

00:38:45.820 --> 00:38:50.490
Let's see if this works.

00:38:50.490 --> 00:38:51.490
OK.

00:38:51.490 --> 00:38:52.250
Can we switch?

00:38:52.250 --> 00:38:52.840
OK, perfect.

00:38:52.840 --> 00:38:54.950
So what you're
going to see here,

00:38:54.950 --> 00:38:56.950
you can see we have
a Kubernetes engine.

00:38:56.950 --> 00:39:00.610
We have the cluster running
that's fairly large.

00:39:00.610 --> 00:39:03.790
Now we also have both a
browser-based interface

00:39:03.790 --> 00:39:07.510
that we won't be demoing today
for citizen data scientists,

00:39:07.510 --> 00:39:10.750
or subject matter experts, to
go in and build a lot of models.

00:39:10.750 --> 00:39:14.080
But we have a CLI that
a more advanced person

00:39:14.080 --> 00:39:17.000
can go build a lot of models
very, very quickly as well.

00:39:17.000 --> 00:39:21.930
So if I do my CLI on
the left-hand side,

00:39:21.930 --> 00:39:23.790
you can see I can
do a lot of things.

00:39:23.790 --> 00:39:26.660
I can run, log in,
run a few models.

00:39:26.660 --> 00:39:29.000
I can check the
status of the models.

00:39:29.000 --> 00:39:32.600
But we can also do guided build.

00:39:32.600 --> 00:39:35.480
And guided build is
when I don't necessarily

00:39:35.480 --> 00:39:37.730
know much about my problem.

00:39:37.730 --> 00:39:39.830
I was given a data
set by somebody.

00:39:39.830 --> 00:39:42.750
I need to go build a lot of
models with that data set.

00:39:42.750 --> 00:39:45.230
I don't necessarily know
what models I need to build.

00:39:45.230 --> 00:39:47.720
I don't know what techniques
are going to be useful.

00:39:47.720 --> 00:39:50.840
I don't know if there is a
lot of missing data in it.

00:39:50.840 --> 00:39:53.660
I'm not given much information
about that data set.

00:39:53.660 --> 00:39:55.310
And that's a very
standard use case

00:39:55.310 --> 00:39:57.530
as well where somebody
gets dumped with a data set

00:39:57.530 --> 00:39:59.204
and say, go do something.

00:39:59.204 --> 00:40:01.120
Or there might be an
outcome, but they may not

00:40:01.120 --> 00:40:02.880
say how to get to an outcome.

00:40:02.880 --> 00:40:06.770
So about 60% to 80% of
subject matter experts time

00:40:06.770 --> 00:40:10.580
goes into figuring out what
do I do with this data set.

00:40:10.580 --> 00:40:12.470
So this is trying to
figure out how do you

00:40:12.470 --> 00:40:16.550
reduce that 60% to 80% of
your time to maybe 20% to 30%,

00:40:16.550 --> 00:40:19.340
rather than spending 80%
of your time just wallowing

00:40:19.340 --> 00:40:20.930
in the dark.

00:40:20.930 --> 00:40:23.510
Now we already
logged into the CLI.

00:40:23.510 --> 00:40:29.830
And if I go into our
actual Kubernetes engine--

00:40:29.830 --> 00:40:31.700
as usual, the token has expired.

00:40:31.700 --> 00:40:33.620
Let me make sure I get my token.

00:40:55.576 --> 00:40:56.080
OK.

00:40:56.080 --> 00:41:01.390
So we've extended Kubernetes
to have a graph tab that

00:41:01.390 --> 00:41:03.710
will tell you what
services you are running,

00:41:03.710 --> 00:41:05.220
how many replicates
you're running,

00:41:05.220 --> 00:41:07.330
what boards are running,
what tasks are running.

00:41:07.330 --> 00:41:09.371
And we had to this because
without them we really

00:41:09.371 --> 00:41:12.072
couldn't go in and
figure out how to debug.

00:41:12.072 --> 00:41:14.030
Not only how to debug,
really what is the scale

00:41:14.030 --> 00:41:15.020
we are running at.

00:41:15.020 --> 00:41:17.630
The numbers that come out and
you have to go do looking,

00:41:17.630 --> 00:41:21.350
looking at logs takes
phenomenally longer time than

00:41:21.350 --> 00:41:23.930
for us to go look at something
like this and get to an answer

00:41:23.930 --> 00:41:24.800
quickly.

00:41:24.800 --> 00:41:28.440
Now these are all the services
that are running already.

00:41:28.440 --> 00:41:30.940
These are the core services
that you saw in the architecture

00:41:30.940 --> 00:41:36.140
diagram, where this is just the
Applied AI running and waiting

00:41:36.140 --> 00:41:37.860
for somebody to
issue any commands.

00:41:37.860 --> 00:41:40.110
And what you see here
in this big flower

00:41:40.110 --> 00:41:41.460
is all our task services.

00:41:41.460 --> 00:41:44.140
So we have 50 services running,
waiting for any of the tasks

00:41:44.140 --> 00:41:45.320
to come in.

00:41:45.320 --> 00:41:52.360
If I go in here and
say, AI guided build,

00:41:52.360 --> 00:41:55.360
and I'm saying,
use a local file,

00:41:55.360 --> 00:41:57.880
and saying, out of all
the different combinations

00:41:57.880 --> 00:42:01.480
of inputs you can use, use the
top 10 inputs that the model is

00:42:01.480 --> 00:42:03.970
automatically selecting has
to be the most important

00:42:03.970 --> 00:42:05.260
as a first pass.

00:42:05.260 --> 00:42:10.995
So this file, the data
file, has 1,000 variables.

00:42:10.995 --> 00:42:12.620
They're all correlated
with each other.

00:42:12.620 --> 00:42:13.924
That's a real data set.

00:42:13.924 --> 00:42:16.340
And you, as a data scientist
or the subject matter expert,

00:42:16.340 --> 00:42:18.980
can go in and figure
out which variables

00:42:18.980 --> 00:42:21.170
are important before going
and building a model.

00:42:21.170 --> 00:42:25.040
What I'm asking is, go
build all possible models

00:42:25.040 --> 00:42:27.620
for all possible
combinations of variables,

00:42:27.620 --> 00:42:31.130
selecting the first 10
important variables per model.

00:42:31.130 --> 00:42:33.270
That's what this
command would do.

00:42:33.270 --> 00:42:34.900
Hit Enter.

00:42:34.900 --> 00:42:36.330
What it's doing
is it's going off,

00:42:36.330 --> 00:42:39.230
and in this particular
case, it has 200 variables.

00:42:39.230 --> 00:42:43.150
It's running 1,056 total models.

00:42:43.150 --> 00:42:47.140
And you can see it's pushing
off all of those task runs

00:42:47.140 --> 00:42:48.850
to the Kubernetes engine.

00:42:48.850 --> 00:42:50.350
And as soon as the
Kubernetes engine

00:42:50.350 --> 00:42:54.400
gets all of these tasks
launched, it'll then send back.

00:42:54.400 --> 00:42:56.454
And you can see the
graph already updating.

00:42:56.454 --> 00:42:58.120
It's actually faster
on the screen here,

00:42:58.120 --> 00:43:01.510
but each of the color
nodes that are coming up,

00:43:01.510 --> 00:43:03.600
each one of those
nodes are actual models

00:43:03.600 --> 00:43:05.510
that are getting spun up.

00:43:05.510 --> 00:43:09.990
And as we are speaking, we
already spun up 693 models,

00:43:09.990 --> 00:43:12.360
and it's live
updating as we speak

00:43:12.360 --> 00:43:16.200
in terms of going off and
pushing it to GK live off

00:43:16.200 --> 00:43:19.770
of the conference Wi-Fi here.

00:43:19.770 --> 00:43:23.030
So as we are speaking,
we just took a data set,

00:43:23.030 --> 00:43:26.120
pushed it out without knowing
what is actually in there,

00:43:26.120 --> 00:43:27.920
asking the system
going and figuring out

00:43:27.920 --> 00:43:30.320
how many models can you build?

00:43:30.320 --> 00:43:33.020
What are the combination
of inputs that'll actually

00:43:33.020 --> 00:43:34.850
work for each of those models?

00:43:34.850 --> 00:43:37.840
Go build it, and come back
to me and show me it is up.

00:43:37.840 --> 00:43:40.220
And all this running
live on a system

00:43:40.220 --> 00:43:42.220
that's live on Google Cloud.

00:43:42.220 --> 00:43:43.910
And in this
particular case, it's

00:43:43.910 --> 00:43:49.540
now already finished
building about 730 models.

00:43:49.540 --> 00:43:51.290
And the responses that
you're getting back

00:43:51.290 --> 00:43:53.720
on the screen there
are running live

00:43:53.720 --> 00:43:56.210
with all of these containers
getting spawned off

00:43:56.210 --> 00:43:57.920
inside the Kubernetes
engine with all

00:43:57.920 --> 00:43:59.920
of the connections between
the different servers

00:43:59.920 --> 00:44:03.230
is running on a cloud.

00:44:03.230 --> 00:44:05.460
Now can we switch back to the--

00:44:13.518 --> 00:44:16.562
so what just happened
here was this data set.

00:44:16.562 --> 00:44:20.170
When we took this data set
with, in this case, 200

00:44:20.170 --> 00:44:24.010
different variables, with a
combination of correlations

00:44:24.010 --> 00:44:25.600
between them-- the
plot you see here,

00:44:25.600 --> 00:44:28.750
each color is a correlation
between one variable

00:44:28.750 --> 00:44:30.332
and another variable.

00:44:30.332 --> 00:44:32.290
The system went in, looked
at the correlations,

00:44:32.290 --> 00:44:34.750
looked at a lot of the other
heuristics in the data set,

00:44:34.750 --> 00:44:36.940
figured out what models
to build in a variety

00:44:36.940 --> 00:44:39.330
of different techniques,
ran the models,

00:44:39.330 --> 00:44:41.920
and came back with
an answer for you.

00:44:41.920 --> 00:44:47.500
Now to run this scale of
the nodes that ran live

00:44:47.500 --> 00:44:52.300
in a few minutes, the 50
nodes running 4,800 CPUs

00:44:52.300 --> 00:44:55.190
with about 30 terabytes of RAM.

00:44:55.190 --> 00:44:57.190
Yes, you just spun it up
for about five minutes,

00:44:57.190 --> 00:44:58.420
came back down.

00:44:58.420 --> 00:45:02.470
But you saved an engineer
maybe two weeks worth of work.

00:45:02.470 --> 00:45:04.459
And they can actually
go do work on what

00:45:04.459 --> 00:45:06.750
is more meaningful for them
to go ask questions and get

00:45:06.750 --> 00:45:08.640
answers from, rather
than figuring out

00:45:08.640 --> 00:45:11.170
what is this data set
actually telling them.

00:45:11.170 --> 00:45:14.310
And we have a lot of
workflows built in internally

00:45:14.310 --> 00:45:18.960
for us to go get the
acceleration off analytics

00:45:18.960 --> 00:45:20.980
possible internally as well.

00:45:20.980 --> 00:45:25.470
Now we did run this, not on
Wi-Fi, but actually [INAUDIBLE]

00:45:25.470 --> 00:45:27.480
with a proper
internet connection.

00:45:27.480 --> 00:45:30.900
We could run 30,000
models live, loading up

00:45:30.900 --> 00:45:35.340
the Kubernetes cluster that I
just showed you with about 60%

00:45:35.340 --> 00:45:36.880
utilization.

00:45:36.880 --> 00:45:37.380
OK.

00:45:37.380 --> 00:45:39.004
And the graph actually
looks like that.

00:45:39.004 --> 00:45:41.120
It went live in about
2 and 1/2 minutes,

00:45:41.120 --> 00:45:43.680
spinning out all the
30,000 models [INAUDIBLE]..

00:45:43.680 --> 00:45:46.545
The nice thing about this is
not all 30,000 actually succeed.

00:45:46.545 --> 00:45:49.700
A lot of times when you try
to push 30,000 models through,

00:45:49.700 --> 00:45:52.190
a cluster that is of this
size, a lot of those things

00:45:52.190 --> 00:45:54.750
fail that have to be
restarted automatically.

00:45:54.750 --> 00:46:00.480
And having GKE, having the Cloud
native services automatically

00:46:00.480 --> 00:46:02.457
restart helps us
tremendously as well.

00:46:02.457 --> 00:46:05.040
We didn't have to really worry
about the infrastructure at all

00:46:05.040 --> 00:46:06.039
in this particular case.

00:46:09.670 --> 00:46:12.350
So I just want to leave
you with two thoughts.

00:46:12.350 --> 00:46:14.170
One is, we're not
experimenting here.

00:46:14.170 --> 00:46:16.709
We're not really saying this
is an abstract thought maybe

00:46:16.709 --> 00:46:19.000
that will be available in
the future in five years time

00:46:19.000 --> 00:46:20.310
or something like that.

00:46:20.310 --> 00:46:23.350
We're saying industry
is using this today.

00:46:23.350 --> 00:46:25.390
A lot of these
applications may not

00:46:25.390 --> 00:46:28.390
seem like traditional deep
learning applications,

00:46:28.390 --> 00:46:31.390
traditional cloud applications,
but a lot of these applications

00:46:31.390 --> 00:46:33.560
are moving to the cloud
as we speak today.

00:46:33.560 --> 00:46:35.980
And a lot of industries
are actually embracing it,

00:46:35.980 --> 00:46:38.600
not necessarily resisting it.

00:46:38.600 --> 00:46:41.160
And challenges still do exist.

00:46:41.160 --> 00:46:44.370
But the biggest challenge,
of course, is accessibility.

00:46:44.370 --> 00:46:46.532
So everybody says they
have a lot of data.

00:46:46.532 --> 00:46:47.990
But when you go in
and say, can you

00:46:47.990 --> 00:46:50.840
give me the data sets,
that becomes a six

00:46:50.840 --> 00:46:52.150
months to a one-year exercise.

00:46:52.150 --> 00:46:53.900
Because internally,
they have to go figure

00:46:53.900 --> 00:46:57.290
out how to go get the data, who
has access to the data sets.

00:46:57.290 --> 00:46:59.660
And the biggest
hurdle today really

00:46:59.660 --> 00:47:04.320
is not even just the
data sets or the talent.

00:47:04.320 --> 00:47:06.470
It's really the
changing the mindsets.

00:47:06.470 --> 00:47:09.080
Where everybody wants to do
something with Analytics.

00:47:09.080 --> 00:47:11.210
Everybody wants to do
something with cloud.

00:47:11.210 --> 00:47:14.770
Everybody understands that it's
going to be valuable to them.

00:47:14.770 --> 00:47:17.810
It's about how they
contextualize that value

00:47:17.810 --> 00:47:19.617
to their actual context.

00:47:19.617 --> 00:47:20.950
That's the biggest hurdle today.

00:47:20.950 --> 00:47:22.100
So I'll leave you with that.

00:47:22.100 --> 00:47:22.641
Thanks a lot.

00:47:22.641 --> 00:47:23.470
Thank you.

00:47:23.470 --> 00:47:23.970
Thank you.

00:47:25.170 --> 00:47:27.920
[MUSIC PLAYING]

